    "alert_id": "EX3_001",
    "title": "Uncontrolled hypothyroidism likely driving weight gain",
    "message": "Patient has TSH 12.4 mIU/L (target <4.5) with low Free T4, dry skin, periorbital puffiness, delayed ankle reflexes, constipation, and cold intolerance — classic hypothyroidism. She reports often forgetting her levothyroxine. The clinician attributes 9 kg weight gain to DTG and plans to switch, but poorly controlled hypothyroidism is a more likely cause. DTG-associated weight gain is well recognised, but this patient's profound hypothyroid symptoms predate any DTG switch consideration and strongly suggest inadequate thyroid replacement. Switching off DTG without first correcting hypothyroidism risks unnecessary ART change with no resolution of the underlying problem.",
    "issue_type": "plan_review",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Address levothyroxine adherence and up-titrate dose to normalise TSH before attributing weight gain to DTG. Reassess weight and symptoms after thyroid optimisation before considering ART switch.",
    "citations": []
  },
